    {
      "id": "p015b0096",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 96,
      "xMin": 172.417,
      "yMin": 454.237357,
      "xMax": 425.128312,
      "yMax": 463.143922,
      "text": "Figure 4: Illustrations of Fine-tuning BERT on Different Tasks."
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0097",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 97,
      "xMin": 72.0,
      "yMin": 487.935721,
      "xMax": 290.269273,
      "yMax": 552.016366,
      "text": "SST-2 The Stanford Sentiment Treebank is a\nbinary single-sentence classification task consist-\ning of sentences extracted from movie reviews\nwith human annotations of their sentiment (Socher\net al., 2013)."
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0098",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 98,
      "xMin": 72.0,
      "yMin": 563.553721,
      "xMax": 290.269513,
      "yMax": 627.634366,
      "text": "CoLA The Corpus of Linguistic Acceptability is\na binary single-sentence classification task, where\nthe goal is to predict whether an English sentence\nis linguistically “acceptable” or not (Warstadt\net al., 2018)."
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0099",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 99,
      "xMin": 72.0,
      "yMin": 639.172721,
      "xMax": 290.269273,
      "yMax": 716.802366,
      "text": "STS-B The Semantic Textual Similarity Bench-\nmark is a collection of sentence pairs drawn from\nnews headlines and other sources (Cer et al.,\n2017). They were annotated with a score from 1\nto 5 denoting how similar the two sentences are in\nterms of semantic meaning."
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0100",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 100,
      "xMin": 72.0,
      "yMin": 728.339721,
      "xMax": 290.269273,
      "yMax": 765.322366,
      "text": "MRPC Microsoft Research Paraphrase Corpus\nconsists of sentence pairs automatically extracted\nfrom online news sources, with human annotations"
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0101",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 101,
      "xMin": 307.276,
      "yMin": 488.06663,
      "xMax": 525.545273,
      "yMax": 511.368366,
      "text": "for whether the sentences in the pair are semanti-\ncally equivalent (Dolan and Brockett, 2005)."
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0102",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 102,
      "xMin": 307.276,
      "yMin": 524.520721,
      "xMax": 525.545273,
      "yMax": 561.502366,
      "text": "RTE Recognizing Textual Entailment is a bi-\nnary entailment task similar to MNLI, but with\nmuch less training data (Bentivogli et al., 2009). 14"
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0103",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 103,
      "xMin": 307.276,
      "yMin": 574.654721,
      "xMax": 525.545273,
      "yMax": 692.931366,
      "text": "WNLI Winograd NLI is a small natural lan-\nguage inference dataset (Levesque et al., 2011).\nThe GLUE webpage notes that there are issues\nwith the construction of this dataset, 15 and every\ntrained system that’s been submitted to GLUE has\nperformed worse than the 65.1 baseline accuracy\nof predicting the majority class. We therefore ex-\nclude this set to be fair to OpenAI GPT. For our\nGLUE submission, we always predicted the ma-"
    },
    {
      "id": "p015b0104",
      "page": 15,
      "block_index": 104,
      "xMin": 307.276,
      "yMin": 704.379187,
      "xMax": 525.545075,
      "yMax": 764.284061,
      "text": "14\nNote that we only report single-task fine-tuning results\nin this paper. A multitask fine-tuning approach could poten-\ntially push the performance even further. For example, we\ndid observe substantial improvements on RTE from multi-\ntask training with MNLI.\n15\nhttps://gluebenchmark.com/faq"
    }
  ]
